<article class="env-lemma" id="02HC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HC">Lemma <span data-tag="02HC">37.6.5</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. Let $U \subset X$ and $V \subset S$ be open such that $f(U) \subset V$. If $f$ is formally unramified, so is $f|_ U : U \to V$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider a solid diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ U \ar[d]_{f|_ U} &  T \ar[d]^ i \ar[l]^ a \\  V &  T' \ar[l] \ar@{-->}[lu] }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       as in Definition <a href="/tag/02H8" data-tag="02H8">37.6.1</a>. If $f$ is formally ramified, then there exists at most one $S$-morphism $a' : T' \to X$ such that $a'|_ T = a$. Hence clearly there exists at most one such morphism into $U$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>